╭───────────────────────────── Literature Review ──────────────────────────────╮
│ Executive Summary                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│ How do multi-agent systems coordinate and communicate? This review           │
│ investigates the operational, architectural, and cognitive mechanisms that   │
│ govern collective action in contemporary multi-agent systems. Based on a     │
│ synthesized corpus spanning classical agent theory and contemporary          │
│ foundation model architectures, the findings reveal a major paradigm shift:  │
│ coordination has migrated from formal, symbolic communication protocols      │
│ rooted in mental state representations toward natural language               │
│ message-passing and conversational turn-taking. Coordination architectures   │
│ bifurcate into centralized or hierarchical orchestration—which enforces      │
│ deterministic control pipelines and dynamic group chats—and decentralized    │
│ peer-to-peer topologies where collective behavior emerges from local         │
│ memory-driven interactions. To sustain collective reasoning across extended  │
│ horizons, systems deploy structured initialization through inception         │
│ prompting, programmatic reply loops, and episodic memory stream mechanisms.  │
│ However, this reliance on statistical language interfaces introduces         │
│ critical failure modes: inter-agent communication channels remain vulnerable │
│ to compounding forward error propagation, cognitive drift over extended      │
│ execution traces, and ungrounded execution loops. The key caveat of this     │
│ literature is that modern systems substitute prompt-engineered behavioral    │
│ conditioning for formal coordination guarantees; lacking verifiably binding  │
│ commitments, collaborative multi-agent execution remains inherently brittle. │
│                                                                              │
│ Introduction                                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│ Understanding how autonomous agents communicate and coordinate is            │
│ fundamental to scaling distributed artificial intelligence. As artificial    │
│ intelligence transitions from isolated models executing single-turn prompts  │
│ toward complex ecosystems of collaborative entities, the mechanisms          │
│ governing inter-agent interactions dictate system efficiency, task           │
│ performance, and safety. Classical multi-agent systems research addressed    │
│ these dynamics through explicit symbolic coordination protocols, intentional │
│ state modeling, and pre-negotiated social conventions. With the rise of      │
│ large language models, however, the coordination substrate has transformed   │
│ into natural language dialogue, allowing agents to negotiate, plan, and      │
│ delegate responsibilities dynamically.                                       │
│                                                                              │
│ This review examines how collective problem-solving is organized,            │
│ structured, and sustained across computational agents. The scope encompasses │
│ the theoretical foundations of agency, conversational message-passing        │
│ frameworks, structural topologies (hierarchical versus peer-to-peer), and    │
│ the cognitive scaffolding that preserves alignment across agent              │
│ interactions. By analyzing the mechanisms through which agents decompose     │
│ tasks, synchronize information, and navigate disagreements, this review      │
│ addresses the central research question: How do multi-agent systems          │
│ coordinate and communicate?                                                  │
│                                                                              │
│ Methodology                                                                  │
│                                                                              │
│ This literature review evaluates evidence drawn from a fixed corpus of 15    │
│ academic papers focused on artificial intelligence agents, reasoning         │
│ strategies, retrieval mechanisms, and collaborative architectures. The       │
│ corpus comprises theoretical foundations of agenthood, foundational          │
│ prompt-based reasoning paradigms, tool-augmented execution frameworks,       │
│ retrieval-augmented generation methodologies, and empirical multi-agent      │
│ conversation architectures.                                                  │
│                                                                              │
│ Retrieval across the corpus was conducted using semantic passage search      │
│ mapped against five targeted sub-questions. These sub-questions examined     │
│ theoretical definitions, message-passing protocols, collective reasoning and │
│ consensus procedures, structural interaction topologies, and coordination    │
│ failure modes. Relevant excerpts were extracted, cataloged within an         │
│ evidence log, and synthesized into thematic clusters based on methodological │
│ commonalities and comparative findings.                                      │
│                                                                              │
│ A methodological limitation of this approach is the reliance on a            │
│ predefined, fixed corpus. Because the corpus was fixed prior to analysis,    │
│ several specialized single-agent reasoning frameworks in the collection      │
│ yielded minimal multi-agent coordination data, while certain broader         │
│ multi-agent benchmarking studies were represented only in localized          │
│ passages. Consequently, the review reflects the design paradigms and         │
│ trade-offs documented within the indexed corpus rather than an exhaustive    │
│ index of the broader distributed artificial intelligence literature.         │
│                                                                              │
