│ loops (Wu et al., 2023). Mitigating these hazards requires the introduction  │
│ of specialized intermediary agents—such as dedicated safeguard modules that  │
│ review generated code for security vulnerabilities prior to execution—as     │
│ well as human-in-the-loop interception mechanisms (Wu et al., 2023).         │
│ Consequently, multi-agent communication cannot operate as a purely           │
│ unconstrained channel; it requires continuous defensive monitoring to        │
│ prevent catastrophic system-wide failure.                                    │
│                                                                              │
│ Discussion                                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│ The analyzed literature demonstrates robust convergence on several core      │
│ principles of multi-agent system design. Across both centralized and         │
│ decentralized paradigms, researchers agree that role specialization is       │
│ essential for effective collective problem-solving (Li et al., 2023; Park et │
│ al., 2023; Wu et al., 2023). Decomposing complex tasks across specialized    │
│ personas—whether an assistant paired with a user proxy (Wu et al., 2023),    │
│ specialized domain experts configured via task specifiers (Li et al., 2023), │
│ or uniquely parameterized social simulacra (Park et al., 2023)—consistently  │
│ yields higher task fidelity than monolithic prompting. Furthermore,          │
│ frameworks agree that natural language serves effectively as a universal     │
│ interface, functioning simultaneously as the coordination protocol, the      │
│ computational transport layer, and the semantic context for tool invocation  │
│ (Li et al., 2023; Wu et al., 2023).                                          │
│                                                                              │
│ However, significant conceptual and architectural tensions divide these      │
│ methodologies. The primary divergence exists between structured              │
│ task-oriented orchestration and emergent social interaction. Frameworks      │
│ designed for computational workflows favor centralized, deterministic        │
│ control structures where a coordinator orchestrates turns and enforces       │
│ rigorous safety bounds (Wu et al., 2023). Conversely, exploratory simulation │
│ frameworks dispense with centralized oversight entirely, relying on          │
│ autonomous reflection and local perception to drive coordination (Park et    │
│ al., 2023). This architectural divergence reflects an unaligned optimization │
│ goal: the former prioritizes verifiability, code safety, and goal            │
│ convergence, whereas the latter prioritizes behavioral plausibility and      │
│ emergent coordination.                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│ A deeper theoretical tension exists between classical multi-agent theory and │
│ modern foundation model implementations. Classical theory emphasizes that    │
│ robust coordination requires formal commitments and explicit mental state    │
│ tracking (Wooldridge & Jennings, 1995). Modern implementations, however,     │
│ replace explicit symbolic commitments with in-context persona emulation and  │
│ conversational prompting (Li et al., 2023; Wu et al., 2023). Because         │
│ foundation model agents lack permanent, verifiable commitment stores, their  │
│ adherence to a joint plan depends entirely on transient context window       │
│ contents and prompt sensitivity. This fundamental difference explains why    │
│ cascading errors (Xi et al., 2023) and cognitive drift (Park et al., 2023)   │
│ occur frequently in modern multi-agent dialogue loops: probabilistic token   │
│ generation provides no mathematical guarantee of convention compliance.      │
│                                                                              │
│ Several critical gaps remain unaddressed in the examined evidence. First,    │
│ while frameworks describe operational mechanics, there is a marked absence   │
│ of quantitative evaluations regarding communication complexity, token        │
│ consumption overhead, and latency bottlenecks when scaling systems beyond    │
│ small agent groups. A plausible implication is that naive group chat         │
│ broadcasting imposes exponential context growth, rendering large-scale       │
│ multi-agent communication economically and computationally impractical       │
│ without aggressive context compression. Second, current frameworks lack      │
│ standardized recovery protocols for halting or rolling back cascaded errors  │
│ once invalid outputs are broadcast across an agent network. While modular    │
│ verification roles exist (Wu et al., 2023), the literature offers few formal │
│ mechanisms for distributed error reconciliation when an intermediate agent   │
│ introduces subtle hallucinations.                                            │
│                                                                              │
│ For engineers building multi-agent systems, these trade-offs suggest that    │
│ natural language communication should not be treated as a fully reliable     │
│ coordination mechanism. Unrestricted peer-to-peer communication should be    │
│ constrained in favor of hierarchical or staged topologies with explicit      │
│ programmatic state machines (Wu et al., 2023). Furthermore, robust           │
│ architectures must implement aggressive memory synthesis pipelines (Park et  │
│ al., 2023) and dedicated verification nodes (Wu et al., 2023) to limit       │
│ forward error cascading.                                                     │
│                                                                              │
│ Conclusion                                                                   │
│                                                                              │
│ Multi-agent systems coordinate and communicate primarily through natural     │
│ language message-passing structured across specialized agent roles. Unlike   │
│ classical architectures that coordinated via symbolic conventions and formal │
│ mental state representations, contemporary frameworks model collaboration as │
│ iterative conversational programming. Structural execution bifurcates        │
│ between centralized orchestrators that route messages through dynamic group  │
│ managers or command hierarchies, and decentralized peer-to-peer topologies   │
│ where collective coordination emerges via local memory retrieval,            │
│ reflection, and planning loops.                                              │
│                                                                              │
│ Nevertheless, significant open challenges constrain the field. Modern        │
│ coordination remains structurally fragile due to cascading error propagation │
│ across agent pipelines, long-horizon coherence loss, and the lack of         │
│ verifiable execution commitments. Addressing these limitations will require  │
│ developing standardized error-recovery protocols, establishing formal        │
│ consensus mechanisms within conversational contexts, and bounding the severe │
│ token overhead of multi-agent communication networks.                        │
